<TopicPage slug="subjects/social-work/foundational/the-social-work-profession">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Social work is <strong>a practice profession with a built-in conscience</strong>. Its mission is
    double-barrelled: to help individuals and families function better, <em>and</em> to change the
    conditions — poverty, discrimination, exclusion — that damage them. That pairing separates it
    from neighbouring professions. Psychology centres the individual mind; psychiatry treats
    disorder; social work starts from the <em>person-in-environment</em>, the conviction that
    private trouble and public issue are two views of the same thing. A worker might spend a
    morning arranging an elder's discharge plan and an afternoon testifying about the housing
    shortage that produced the crisis — and count both as the same job.
  </p>
  <p>
    The profession organises that job through <strong>generalist practice</strong>: a single
    problem-solving method applied at whatever scale the situation demands. And it disciplines the
    whole enterprise through the <strong>NASW Code of Ethics</strong>, one of the most detailed
    ethical codes of any profession.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="from mission to method: how the profession fits together" widget="equationStep" config={swCfg} />

  <h2>History and mission</h2>
  <p>
    Modern social work has two parent traditions. The <strong>casework</strong> line descends from
    organised charity — volunteers with ledgers who pioneered the radical idea that help should be
    systematic, recorded and aimed at lasting change. The <strong>settlement</strong> line descends
    from reformers who moved into poor neighbourhoods: Hull House ran kindergartens, labour
    organising and the research that fed into federal policy. Casework contributed method;
    settlements contributed the justice agenda. The twentieth century married them — schools of
    social work appeared, the New Deal made the profession an instrument of the welfare state, and
    in 1955 the National Association of Social Workers gave it one professional voice.
  </p>
  <p>
    The mission statement the profession settled on keeps both parents: well-being for all people,
    with <em>particular attention</em> to the vulnerable, the oppressed and the poor. Attention to
    the environment, not just the person, is written into the professional DNA.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="milestones: from friendly visitors to a credentialed profession" widget="timeline" config={swTl} />

  <h2>The NASW Code of Ethics</h2>
  <p>
    The Code does three things at once. It <strong>names the values</strong> (service, social
    justice, dignity and worth of the person, importance of human relationships, integrity,
    competence). It <strong>translates them into standards</strong> — on confidentiality, informed
    consent, boundaries, dual relationships, cultural competence, record-keeping, supervision. And
    it <strong>grapples with the profession's hardest tensions</strong>: workers answer to clients,
    to agencies, to the law and to society, and the Code explicitly recognises that these
    loyalties can conflict. Ethical decision-making in social work is therefore a process, not a
    lookup table — identify the conflict, consult the Code, consult supervision, weigh who is
    most at risk, document the reasoning.
  </p>

  <h2>Generalist practice</h2>
  <p>
    Generalist practice is the profession's core technology: one cycle —
    <strong>engage, assess, plan, intervene, evaluate, terminate and follow up</strong> — usable at
    any scale. At the <em>micro</em> level it is casework: a biopsychosocial assessment, a service
    plan, crisis response. At the <em>mezzo</em> level it is group work and organisational
    practice. At the <em>macro</em> level it is community organising and policy advocacy. The
    generalist is not a dilettante in all three; it is one worker who can <em>diagnose</em> at which
    level intervention will actually help, and who understands how a change at one level ripples
    into the others — the systems thinking that makes "person-in-environment" operational.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> NASW Code of Ethics (2021 revision); CSWE Educational Policy and Accreditation Standards (2022 EPAS); Popple &amp; Leighninger, The Policy-Based Profession; Richmond, Social Diagnosis (1917).</p>
